{
  "dom_id": "step-q330630",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2327\\,$ | $\\,0.2296\\,$ | $\\,0.2266\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2643\\,$ | $\\,0.2611\\,$ | $\\,0.2578\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.2981\\,$ | $\\,0.2946\\,$ | $\\,0.2912\\,$ |\n\nThe data above is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z>0.64).$ Use the table above and do not round your answer.\n\n$P(Z>0.64)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/d59151ff19329029e209bd271c992c775777939ed0a670c4ce73e0985328bf6b.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ we have the following symmetry property for $a>0:$\n\n$$\nP(Z>a)=P(Z<-a)=Φ(-a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z>0.64).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/a16528d20a4a8182b6f5898def6f1337450b7fe6d0305644ed214237716d47ae.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z<-0.64).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/eb2863fc4ff489cf258e6d80c44efc3c4d6ac7b08be5c71260feb8813934bc6d.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z>0.64)=P(Z<-0.64)=Φ(-0.64)=0.2611.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
